<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Wellness and Mindfulness – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Wellness and Mindfulness</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> The Benefits of Mindfulness</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td colspan="3" style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td colspan="4" style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Resolve misunderstandings in peer interactions. Concept: Interpret graphical information for classmates.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can help peers understand wellness activities by demonstrating exercises with body language (e.g., shows a stretching exercise while explaining its purpose). Can interpret graphs on exercise benefits by pointing out data and explaining it in simple terms, using familiar extracurricular activities in Panama (e.g., points to the bar on the chart that shows how students' energy improved after practicing baseball or folkloric dance, and explains how the graph shows students' stress levels decreased after participating in track and field or afternoon meditation sessions). Can clarify wellness terms by using both English and familiar language (e.g., describes "mindfulness" in simple terms in both languages).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a simple infographic with icons representing different wellness activities (e.g., a person meditating, someone exercising, a fruit salad). Students work in pairs to describe what each icon represents and its benefit, using familiar vocabulary. The teacher encourages them to clarify any terms for their partner using simple English or a familiar word if necessary (e.g., "This shows 'meditation,' it's like 'relajación' to calm your mind").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to explain one icon from the infographic. "This icon shows 'exercise.' It helps to *boost* your *energy* and *strengthen* your *body*. When you exercise, you *are maintaining* your physical *health*." The teacher highlights collocations (boost energy, strengthen body) and the use of present simple/continuous. The teacher also explicitly models stress in multisyllabic words like "ex-er-cise" and "flex-i-bil-i-ty" while describing a stretch.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'meditation *boosts focus*', what does 'boosts focus' mean? Does it make your focus go up or down?" (Checks understanding of collocation).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If someone doesn't understand 'resilience,' how can you explain it simply?" (Checks ability to mediate terms).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Wellness and Mindfulness".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a simple bar graph titled "Student Wellness Survey: Benefits of Mindfulness Practice" showing hypothetical data for 9th graders from different Panamanian schools who participated in mindfulness sessions. The bars could represent "Improvement in Focus," "Reduction in Stress," and "Increase in Energy." The data could show, for example, 70% improved focus, 60% reduced stress, and 50% increased energy. The teacher will also have a short audio clip of a peer describing a simple stretching exercise and its benefits.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Engage in group discussions on wellness routines, contributing suggestions and solutions to promote mental and physical well-being. Interpret graphical information to communicate findings to others.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the audio clip and look at the graph.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main idea of this graph? What is the main idea of the audio clip?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "True or False: The graph shows that mindfulness helps students *reduce stress* more than it *boosts energy*." (Students justify their answer by pointing to the graph).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> "Match the benefit from the audio (e.g., 'improves flexibility') with a general category from the graph (e.g., 'physical well-being')."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice interpreting graphical information and explaining wellness activities using target grammar (Present continuous, Present simple), vocabulary (wellness, mindfulness, benefits, energy, stress, focus, flexibility), and collocations (energy boost, improve focus, reduce stress, strengthen body). Practice clarifying terms and using body language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Graph Interpretation Sentences:</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides sentence frames related to the graph from Stage 2. Students complete the sentences and then explain their answer to their partner. E.g., "The graph shows that ___% of students experienced an _________ in focus. This means mindfulness can help to _______ your brain." (Possible answers: 70, improvement, boost/enhance). They must point to the relevant bar on the graph as they explain.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled - Wellness Activity Explanation & Demonstration:</b> Students receive cards with different wellness activities (e.g., "Deep Breathing," "Gentle Stretching," "Mindful Walk"). In pairs, one student chooses a card and explains the activity and its benefits to their partner, using target vocabulary (e.g., "This activity is called 'Gentle Stretching.' It helps to *improve flexibility* and *relax* your muscles. See, I *am stretching* my arms right now. It can also *boost* your *strength* over time."). The student must demonstrate the exercise with simple body language and clarify any terms their partner finds confusing (e.g., "Flexibility means you can move your body easily, como ser 'flexible' en español."). The teacher monitors for correct use of present simple/continuous and collocations, and provides feedback on pronunciation, especially stress in words like "flexible" and "resilience."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation Focus - Minimal Pairs & Stress:</b> The teacher leads a quick drill focusing on minimal pairs /ʊ/ and /uː/ in context: "He *could* wear a *suit* to the meeting. He *cooed* at the baby while cleaning the *soot* from the chimney." Students repeat after the teacher, then practice in pairs. The teacher also points out and drills stress in words like "mind-FUL-ness," "re-SIL-ience," and "flex-i-BIL-i-ty" as students review their activity explanations.</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Wellness and Mindfulness".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Group Presentation/Workshop Segment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students work in their "Wellness Workshop" groups from the 21st Century Skills Project. They are tasked with preparing and presenting a 2-3 minute segment of their workshop to another peer group, focusing on demonstrating, explaining, and clarifying. This segment should cover one wellness activity and its benefits, incorporating graphical data.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups of 3-4 students prepare to present one specific wellness activity (e.g., "Mindful Breathing," "Desk Yoga," "Hydration Habit").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group is provided with or creates a simple graphic/chart (e.g., "Benefits of Daily Hydration" showing "energy boost," "improved focus," "better skin"). The graphic can show simple percentages or a rating scale.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">During the presentation, students must:
                            <ol>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Introduce their wellness activity.</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrate the activity using body language (e.g., showing a stretching exercise or breathing technique) while explaining its purpose and benefits using present simple ("This practice *promotes* *relaxation*") and present continuous ("We *are practicing* a gentle stretch now").</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Interpret their graphic, pointing to data and explaining it in simple terms, using collocations (e.g., "Our graph shows that 80% of students felt an *energy boost* after drinking more water"). They can relate it to Panamanian activities like baseball or folkloric dance if applicable for energy.</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Actively resolve misunderstandings and clarify wellness terms for their peer audience, using simple English and familiar language if needed (e.g., "When we say 'mental well-being,' we mean how happy and calm your mind is, la 'salud mental'").</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Engage in brief Q&A, offering suggestions and solutions related to the activity.</li>
                            </ol>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, offering support and noting effective mediation strategies and language use.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their Wellness Workshop segment to the class or a rotating peer group. Peers use a simple checklist to evaluate how well the presenting group mediated information.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist & Peer Feedback):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Explanation:</b> Were the wellness activities and benefits explained clearly?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interpretation of Graphical Information:</b> Did the group accurately point out data and explain it in simple terms?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Demonstration with Body Language:</b> Was body language used effectively to show exercises and their purpose?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarification of Terms:</b> Did the group clarify wellness terms effectively, using simple English or familiar language when needed?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Language:</b> Did the group use present simple, present continuous, and collocations with health-related verbs (e.g., energy boost, improve focus)?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Was pronunciation intelligible, with attention to stress in multisyllabic words?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one thing you did well when explaining your wellness activity or graph to your classmates?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one challenge you faced when trying to make your classmates understand, and how did you try to resolve it?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel now in helping a friend understand the benefits of mindfulness or a wellness activity?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of today's lesson helped you most in practicing your mediation skills?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What strategies worked well for students to mediate information (explaining, demonstrating, clarifying)? Were there any common misunderstandings or difficulties in interpreting the graph or explaining wellness terms? How effectively did students use present simple, present continuous, and health-related collocations? What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for future mediation tasks, especially in integrating visual aids and verbal explanation?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "Benefits of Mindfulness" theme. The mediation skills practiced will be essential for students to effectively present their full "Wellness Workshop" project, applying these communication and interpretation skills in a broader context for subsequent project-based learning units.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">2-3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>